<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Donato Bramante</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1444">1444</a>–<a href="/years/1514">1514</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/15th-century">15th century</a> · Also known as Bramante</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Donato Bramante was an Italian architect and painter, born near Urbino, who worked at the Sforza court in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/milan">Milan</a> from the late 1470s until 1499, building the church of Santa Maria presso San Satiro and the domed tribune of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/santa-maria-delle-grazie-milan">Santa Maria delle Grazie</a>. In <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> he became the leading architect of the High <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/renaissance">Renaissance</a>, designing the Tempietto and the first plan of the new St Peter's Basilica for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pope-julius-ii">Pope Julius II</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/donato-bramante.jpg" alt="Donato Bramante" width="320" height="437" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Donato_Bramante.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Bramante was born Donato di Pascuccio d'Antonio and is also known as Bramante Lazzari. He was born at Fermignano near Urbino, where in 1467 Luciano Laurana was adding an arcaded courtyard and other <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/renaissance">Renaissance</a> features to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/federico-da-montefeltro">Federico da Montefeltro</a>'s ducal palace. He knew the painters Melozzo da Forlì and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/piero-della-francesca">Piero della Francesca</a>, who were interested in perspective. His architecture has eclipsed his painting.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>Around 1474 Bramante moved to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/milan">Milan</a>, a city with a deep Gothic tradition, and built several churches in the new antique style. From 1476 Duke <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ludovico-sforza">Ludovico Sforza</a> made him virtually his court architect. His commissions culminated in the trompe-l'œil choir of Santa Maria presso San Satiro, built in 1482 to 1486. Space was limited, so he made a theatrical apse in bas-relief that combines perspective with Roman details. He also built the tribune of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/santa-maria-delle-grazie-milan">Santa Maria delle Grazie</a> in 1492 to 1499 and the cloisters of Sant'Ambrogio in 1497 to 1498, and worked on Pavia Cathedral, setting the design and creating the crypt and part of the apse.</p><p>In 1499 the French army drove out his Sforza patron, and Bramante went to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a>, where Cardinal Riario already knew him. Cardinal <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/della-rovere">Della Rovere</a>, soon to be <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pope-julius-ii">Pope Julius II</a>, took notice of him. He designed the cloister of Santa Maria della Pace in 1500 to 1504 and in 1502 the Tempietto of San Pietro in Montorio, built over the spot where Peter was martyred. Though small, it has the strict proportions and symmetry of classical buildings, with slender Doric columns and a dome, and it marked the beginning of the High Renaissance in Rome.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Works and ideas</h2><p>In November 1503 Julius II engaged him for the complete rebuilding of St Peter's Basilica, the grandest architectural commission in 16th-century <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a>. The cornerstone of the first great pier of the crossing was laid on 17 April 1506. Bramante planned a centralised Greek cross, which symbolised sublime perfection for him and his generation, with four chapels filling the corners between equal transepts, each capped with a smaller dome around the great dome. The plan was more Romano-Byzantine in form than the church built, and it was fundamentally altered when the nave was extended after his death. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/michelangelo">Michelangelo</a> later executed a design based on it.</p><p>He also planned the Cortile del Belvedere, a vast courtyard linking the buildings of the Vatican, and designed the Palazzo Caprini. Very few of his drawings survive, though some by his assistants do. He wrote about architecture and composed eighty sonnets. He died on 11 April 1514.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Donato Bramante” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote><b>Donato Bramante</b> built the tribune of Santa Maria delle Grazie, and the University of Pavia, founded under the Visconti in 1361, trained lawyers and physicians for the duchy.</blockquote><a href="/empire/duchy-of-milan">Duchy of Milan · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>According to Giorgio Vasari, Julius II was provoked by <b>Bramante</b> to insist.</blockquote><a href="/work/sistine-chapel-ceiling">Sistine Chapel ceiling · How It Was Made</a></li><li><blockquote>A competition was held under Julius II, and <b>Bramante</b>'s design was chosen.</blockquote><a href="/work/st-peters-basilica">St. Peter's Basilica · How It Was Made</a></li><li><blockquote>Pope Julius II laid the first stone of <b>Bramante</b>'s new basilica in Rome.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/april-18">18 April in history</a></li><li><blockquote>Its design involved <b>Bramante</b>, Michelangelo and Maderno over 120 years.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/november-18">18 November in history</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Fermignano, in what is now <a href="/countries/italy">Italy</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/italy">Italy</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=43.68,12.65&amp;year=1489" aria-label="Fermignano on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4854 616 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Donato Bramante"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5304 886h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5304 886h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=43.68,12.65&amp;year=1489">What was here in 1489? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
